(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test07_2B"] = [{"id":"6d5ddea4","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"According to Potawatomi ecologist Robin Wall Kimmerer, the Indigenous method of harvesting Hierochloe odorata, or sweetgrass, by snapping the plant off at the root actually ______ wild populations: it may seem counterintuitive, she says, but this method of removal allows new sweetgrass plants to repopulate the space, with an overall increase in number and vigor.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["selects","originates","conditions","replenishes"]},{"id":"5127fc1e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The north celestial pole (NCP) — the fixed point around which stars in the Northern Hemisphere (including the Sun) appear to rotate — is discernible only at night. Inspired by the navigational strategies of some insects and birds, researchers devised a method for locating the NCP in daytime using skylight polarization, which occurs as atmospheric particles scatter sunlight. A polarimetric camera captures images of polarization patterns, which rotate as the Sun’s position in the sky changes; temporal variances across images can then be used to determine an observer’s latitude and bearing relative to the NCP.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The north celestial pole (NCP)—the fixed point around which stars in the Northern Hemisphere (including the Sun) appear to rotate—is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"discernible only at night. Inspired by the navigational strategies of some insects and birds, researchers devised a method for locating the NCP in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"daytime using skylight polarization, which occurs as atmospheric particles scatter sunlight. A polarimetric camera captures images of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"polarization patterns, which rotate as the Sun’s position in the sky changes; temporal variances across images can then be used to determine an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"observer’s latitude and bearing relative to the NCP.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It illustrates how most navigational tools utilize the NCP, recounts how researchers discovered that certain animals are able to navigate without using the NCP, and then proposes that this discovery could be used to avoid problems in navigation associated with reliance on the NCP.","It presents a celestial-based method of navigation, enumerates the comparative benefits of an alternative method used by certain animals that is based on an unrelated natural occurrence, and then indicates how researchers assessed the relative accuracy of the two methods.","It explains how the NCP is typically located, emphasizes a key difference between how humans and certain animals use the NCP for navigation, and then suggests an alternative way of using the NCP to improve existing navigational instruments.","It notes an obstacle to observing an astronomical phenomenon, mentions a navigational ability of certain animals that inspired a solution to that obstacle, and then explains how researchers used an optical device to mimic that ability."]},{"id":"490b205f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In editor Lisa Yaszek’s introduction to her anthology The Future Is Female! More Classic Science Fiction Stories by Women, Yaszek identifies an increasing sense of ______ feminist mode of writing in the 1970s, in contrast to many woman-authored science fiction stories of the 1920s to 1960s whose politics were less deliberately signaled.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In editor Lisa Yaszek’s introduction to her anthology The Future Is Female! More Classic Science Fiction Stories by Women, Yaszek identifies an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"increasing sense of ______ feminist mode of writing in the 1970s, in contrast to many woman-authored science fiction stories of the 1920s to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"1960s whose politics were less deliberately signaled.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["a prudently","an overtly","a cordially","an inadvertently"]},{"id":"1fbf276a","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Interruptions in the supply chain for microchips used in personal electronics have challenged an economist’s assertion that retailers can expect robust growth in sales of those devices in the coming months. The delays are unlikely to ______ her projection entirely but will almost certainly extend its time frame.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Interruptions in the supply chain for microchips used in personal electronics have challenged an economist’s assertion that retailers can expect","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"robust growth in sales of those devices in the coming months. The delays are unlikely to ______ her projection entirely but will almost certainly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"extend its time frame.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["dispute","withdraw","underscore","invalidate"]},{"id":"749f3334","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from Charlotte Forten Grimké s 1888 poem “At Newport.” Oh, deep delight to watch the gladsome waves Exultant leap upon the rugged rocks; Ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on — Filled with a life that will not know defeat; To see the glorious hues of sky and sea. The distant snowy sails, glide spirit like, Into an unknown world, to feel the sweet Enchantment of the sea thrill all the soul, Clearing the clouded brain, making the heart Leap joyous as it own bright, singing waves!","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Charlotte Forten Grimké’s 1888 poem “At Newport.”","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Oh, deep delight to watch the gladsome waves","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Exultant leap upon the rugged rocks;","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Filled with a life that will not know defeat;","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"To see the glorious hues of sky and sea.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The distant snowy sails, glide spirit like,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Into an unknown world, to feel the sweet","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Enchantment of the sea thrill all the soul,","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Clearing the clouded brain, making the heart","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Leap joyous as it own bright, singing waves!","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","choices":["It portrays the surroundings as an imposing and intimidating scene.","It characterizes the sea’s waves as a relentless and enduring force.","It conveys the speaker’s ambivalence about the natural world.","It draws a contrast between the sea’s waves and the speaker’s thoughts."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Ever repulsed, yet ever rushing on—","Filled with a life that will not know defeat;"]},{"id":"97ab5669","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Former astronaut Ellen Ochoa says that although she doesn’t have a definite idea of when it might happen, she ______ that humans will someday need to be able to live in other environments than those found on Earth. This conjecture informs her interest in future research missions to the moon.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Former astronaut Ellen Ochoa says that although she doesn’t have a definite idea of when it might happen, she ______ that humans will someday","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"need to be able to live in other environments than those found on Earth. This conjecture informs her interest in future research missions to the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"moon.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["demands","speculates","doubts","establishes"]},{"id":"9b2fbb2e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Logically, a damaged fossil should provide less information than an intact one, but for paleontologist Brigitte Schoenemann, a broken area on a fossilized trilobite (a crustacean-like creature) ______ fresh insight, allowing her to view the inner structure of the organism’s eye.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Logically, a damaged fossil should provide less information than an intact one, but for paleontologist Brigitte Schoenemann, a broken area on a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"fossilized trilobite (a crustacean-like creature) ______ fresh insight, allowing her to view the inner structure of the organism’s eye.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["resolved","adjusted","offered","directed"]},{"id":"cb526866","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The recent discovery of a carved wooden figure dating to around 2,000 years ago in a ditch in England was truly surprising. Wooden objects ______ survive for so long due to their high susceptibility to rot, but archaeologists suspect layers of sediment in the ditch preserved the figure by creating an oxygen-free environment.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The recent discovery of a carved wooden figure dating to around 2,000 years ago in a ditch in England was truly surprising. Wooden objects","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______ survive for so long due to their high susceptibility to rot, but archaeologists suspect layers of sediment in the ditch preserved the figure by","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"creating an oxygen-free environment.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["sturdily","carelessly","rarely","simply"]},{"id":"01989d77","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Microbes that live in shallow lakes and ponds produce methane, a harmful greenhouse gas. Ecologist Ralf Aben and his team wanted to see how different types of shallow-water plants might affect the amount of methane that escapes into the atmosphere. Aben’s team set up some water tanks with soil and microbes from local ponds. Some tanks had a type of underwater plant that grows in the soil called watermilfoil. Other tanks had either duckweed, a type of plant that floats on the water’s surface, or algae. Aben and his team found that tanks with duckweed and algae released higher levels of methane than tanks with watermilfoil did. This finding suggests that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Microbes that live in shallow lakes and ponds produce methane, a harmful greenhouse gas. Ecologist Ralf Aben and his team wanted to see how","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"different types of shallow-water plants might affect the amount of methane that escapes into the atmosphere. Aben’s team set up some water","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"tanks with soil and microbes from local ponds. Some tanks had a type of underwater plant that grows in the soil called watermilfoil. Other tanks","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"had either duckweed, a type of plant that floats on the water’s surface, or algae. Aben and his team found that tanks with duckweed and algae","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"released higher levels of methane than tanks with watermilfoil did. This finding suggests that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the presence of some kinds of underwater plants like watermilfoil helps prevent methane from escaping shallow lakes and ponds.","shallow lakes and ponds release more methane than deeper bodies of water because shallow bodies of water usually have more plants than deep bodies of water do.","shallow lakes and ponds are more likely to contain algae than to contain either watermilfoil or duckweed.","having a mix of algae, underwater plants, and floating plants is the best way to reduce the amount of methane in shallow lakes and ponds."]},{"id":"c4bee178","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Moons of Dwarf Planets Dwarf planet name Number of moons Name of moons Haumea 2 Hi‘iaka, Namaka Ceres 0 N/A Makemake 1 MK 2 Eris 1 Dysnomia Pluto 5 Charon, Nix, Kerberos, Styx, Hydra Like Earth, some dwarf planets in the solar system have exactly one moon. Two examples of such dwarf planets are ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"","headers":["Moons of Dwarf Planets"],"rows":[["0                         N/A1                         MK 2"],["1                      Dysnomia5          Charon, Nix, Kerberos, Styx, Hydra"]]},{"type":"text","text":"Moons of Dwarf Planets Dwarf planet name Number of moons Name of moons Haumea 2 Hi‘iaka, Namaka Ceres 0 N/A Makemake 1 MK 2 Eris 1 Dysnomia Pluto 5 Charon, Nix, Kerberos, Styx, Hydra Like Earth, some dwarf planets in the solar system have exactly one moon. Two examples of such dwarf planets are ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["Eris and Makemake.","Haumea and Eris.","Pluto and Haumea.","Makemake and Ceres."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Moons of Dwarf","Planets"]},{"id":"356ad646","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Baltimore, Maryland, has installed engineered structures along 71% of its shoreline to protect infrastructure from wave erosion and other hazards, a practice known as shoreline hardening. To evaluate the responses of waterbirds to two types of hardening structures — riprap and bulkheads — Diann Prosser et al. surveyed waterbird communities consisting of the tundra swan, the great blue heron, and 62 other species at different sites in the Chesapeake Bay on the US East Coast. Utilizing the Index of Waterbird Community Integrity (IWCI), on which a high score corresponds to high community integrity,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Baltimore, Maryland, has installed engineered structures along 71% of its shoreline to protect infrastructure from wave erosion and other","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"hazards, a practice known as shoreline hardening. To evaluate the responses of waterbirds to two types of hardening structures—riprap and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"bulkheads—Diann Prosser et al. surveyed waterbird communities consisting of the tundra swan, the great blue heron, and 62 other species at","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"different sites in the Chesapeake Bay on the US East Coast. Utilizing the Index of Waterbird Community Integrity (IWCI), on which a high score","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"corresponds to high community integrity, the researchers found that bulkheads are more strongly negatively correlated with waterbird","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"community integrity than is riprap.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly illustrate the researchers’ finding?","choices":["The difference in average IWCI scores for waterbird communities at Stony and Old Road, two sites with a higher percentage of shoreline consisting of bulkheads than of riprap, was statistically insignificant.","Waterbird communities at Old Road, a site with a relatively high percentage of shoreline consisting of bulkheads, had lower average IWCI scores than did waterbird communities at Miles, a site with a relatively high percentage of shoreline consisting of riprap.","Waterbird communities at Curtis, a site with a high percentage of shoreline consisting of bulkheads and riprap, had lower average IWCI scores than did waterbird communities at Onancock, a site with a low percentage of shoreline consisting of bulkheads and riprap.","Waterbird communities at Curtis, a site with equal percentages of shoreline consisting of bulkheads and riprap, had higher average IWCI scores than did waterbird communities at Miles, a site with different percentages of shoreline consisting of bulkheads and riprap."]},{"id":"9869c261","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Data collected by the Mars rover Curiosity at the Gale Crater’s Murray Formation are suggestive of hydrological deposition of sediment in the distant past. To characterize the nature of the depositional environment, Frances Rivera-Hernández et al. analyzed the grain size of Murray Formation sediment, finding that although there are intervals of coarse grains, most of the sediment consists of fine grains that show signs of cracking due to episodic desiccation. Rivera-Hernández et al. concluded that the coarse grains are sandstone, which tends to be deposited by flowing water, whereas the fine grains are mudstone, which is slowly deposited by settling out of suspension in low-flow water, leading the researchers to posit that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Data collected by the Mars rover Curiosity at the Gale Crater’s Murray Formation are suggestive of hydrological deposition of sediment in the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"distant past. To characterize the nature of the depositional environment, Frances Rivera-Hernández et al. analyzed the grain size of Murray","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Formation sediment, finding that although there are intervals of coarse grains, most of the sediment consists of fine grains that show signs of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cracking due to episodic desiccation. Rivera-Hernández et al. concluded that the coarse grains are sandstone, which tends to be deposited by","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"flowing water, whereas the fine grains are mudstone, which is slowly deposited by settling out of suspension in low-flow water, leading the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"researchers to posit that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["although the area of the Murray Formation experienced a prolonged period of dryness that prevented a lake from forming, water flowing from a distant source was present.","a lake existed at the Murray Formation for a prolonged period, though the lake occasionally experienced drying and there were periods in which one or more streams were present.","one or more streams existed at the Murray Formation for an extended period until being replaced by a lake that persisted for only a brief period before permanently drying.","a stream-fed lake was present at the Murray Formation for an extended period, and although the streams experienced occasional drying, the lake did not."]},{"id":"860803dd","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Biologist Valentina Gómez-Bahamón and her team have investigated two subspecies of the fork-tailed flycatcher bird that live in the same region in Colombia, but one subspecies migrates south for part of the year, and the other doesn’t.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Biologist Valentina Gómez-Bahamón and her team have investigated two subspecies of the fork-tailed flycatcher bird that live in the same region","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in Colombia, but one subspecies migrates south for part of the year, and the other doesn’t. The researchers found that, due to slight differences","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in feather shape, the feathers of migratory forked-tailed flycatcher males make a sound during flight that is higher pitched than that made by the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"feathers of nonmigratory males. The researchers hypothesize that fork-tailed flycatcher females are attracted to the specific sound made by the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"males of their own subspecies, and that over time the females’ preference will drive further genetic and anatomical divergence between the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"subspecies.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Gómez-Bahamón and her team’s hypothesis?","choices":["The feathers located on the wings of the migratory fork-tailed flycatchers have a narrower shape than those of the nonmigratory birds, which allows them to fly long distances.","Over several generations, the sound made by the feathers of migratory male fork-tailed flycatchers grows progressively higher pitched relative to that made by the feathers of nonmigratory males.","Fork-tailed flycatchers communicate different messages to each other depending on whether their feathers create high-pitched or low-pitched sounds.","The breeding habits of the migratory and nonmigratory fork-tailed flycatchers remained generally the same over several generations."]},{"id":"6af80ff3","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Banded Sand Catsharks Found in Three Sponges 18 16 14 Number of sharks 12 10 8 6 4 2 0 e e ed al al in m m fe rm ete d un sponge 1 sponge 2 sponge 3 Researcher Helen O Neill and her team were studying sponges in the family Irciniidae when they discovered that three of the sponges were functioning as microhabitats for banded sand catsharks (Atelomycterus fasciatus). Additionally, the team found that male and female A. fasciatus share the same sponge (the same microhabitat), a behavior not found among Port Jackson sharks (Heterodontus portusjacksoni), another seabed-dwelling species.","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Banded Sand Catsharks Found in Three Sponges — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"Researcher Helen O Neill and her team were studying sponges in the family Irciniidae when they discovered that three of the sponges were functioning as microhabitats for banded sand catsharks (Atelomycterus fasciatus). Additionally, the team found that male and female A. fasciatus share the same sponge (the same microhabitat), a behavior not found among Port Jackson sharks (Heterodontus portusjacksoni), another seabed-dwelling species."}],"question":"Which choice best describes data from the graph that support the underlined claim?","choices":["Each of the three sponges was inhabited by at least one male A. fasciatus and at least one undetermined A. fasciatus.","Sponge 1 was inhabited by 17 male A. fasciatus.","Each of the three sponges was inhabited by at least one male A. fasciatus and at least one female A. fasciatus.","Sponge 1 was inhabited by 13 female A. fasciatus."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["the team found that male and female","A.","fasciatus","share the same sponge (the same microhabitat),"]},{"id":"5432d1de","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"It’s common for jazz musicians and fans to refer to certain songs as having “swing,” indicating that the songs provoke a strong feeling, like the impulse to tap one’s foot or dance. The exact acoustic properties that give a song swing, however, have long been thought to be undefinable. To investigate swing, a team led by physicist Corentin Nelias delayed the downbeats and synchronized the offbeats in jazz piano solos and asked jazz musicians to compare the intensity of swing in each modified piece with the intensity of swing in the original piece. They found that participants were more than seven times likelier to characterize the modified songs as having swing than to characterize the original versions as having swing, suggesting that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"It’s common for jazz musicians and fans to refer to certain songs as having “swing,” indicating that the songs provoke a strong feeling, like the","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"impulse to tap one’s foot or dance. The exact acoustic properties that give a song swing, however, have long been thought to be undefinable. To","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"investigate swing, a team led by physicist Corentin Nelias delayed the downbeats and synchronized the offbeats in jazz piano solos and asked","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"jazz musicians to compare the intensity of swing in each modified piece with the intensity of swing in the original piece. They found that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"participants were more than seven times likelier to characterize the modified songs as having swing than to characterize the original versions as","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"having swing, suggesting that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["synchronized offbeats tend to give a song swing regardless of whether downbeats are delayed.","the acoustic properties that give a song swing are not easy for jazz musicians to manipulate.","jazz songs that feature the piano are more likely to have swing than are jazz songs that do not feature the piano.","the timing of downbeats and offbeats may play a crucial role in giving a song swing."]},{"id":"109d5bbb","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"‘ With some 16,000 in attendance, the Second World Black and African Festival of Arts and ______ or FESTAC 77, as the event was more commonly known — became the largest pan-African event on record. FESTAC drew people from around the world to Lagos, Nigeria, for a monthlong celebration of Black and African art, scholarship, and activism.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"With some 16,000 in attendance, the Second World Black and African Festival of Arts and ______ or FESTAC ‘77, as the event was more","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"commonly known—became the largest pan-African event on record. FESTAC drew people from around the world to Lagos, Nigeria, for a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"monthlong celebration of Black and African art, scholarship, and activism.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Culture:","Culture —","Culture,","Culture"]},{"id":"f30a478e","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"A study published by Rice University geoscientist Ming Tang in 2019 offers a new explanation for the origin of Earth’s ______ structures called arcs, towering ridges that form when a dense oceanic plate subducts under a less dense continental plate, melts in the mantle below, and then rises and bursts through the continental crust above.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A study published by Rice University geoscientist Ming Tang in 2019 offers a new explanation for the origin of Earth’s ______ structures called","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"arcs, towering ridges that form when a dense oceanic plate subducts under a less dense continental plate, melts in the mantle below, and then","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"rises and bursts through the continental crust above.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["continents geological","continents: geological","continents; geological","continents. Geological"]},{"id":"a30567fd","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Nuhād al- Ḥaddād, known as Fairuz, was one of the most beloved Lebanese singers of the twentieth century. Her broad singing repertoire —which included traditional forms, such as the Arabic qasida and maqam, alongside modern pop and jazz styles—lent Fairuz a timeless, cross- generational appeal, ______ her the moniker “the soul of Lebanon.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Nuhād al-Ḥaddād, known as Fairuz, was one of the most beloved Lebanese singers of the twentieth century. Her broad singing repertoire—which","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"included traditional forms, such as the Arabic qasida and maqam, alongside modern pop and jazz styles—lent Fairuz a timeless, cross-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"generational appeal, ______ her the moniker “the soul of Lebanon.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["earned","had earned","earning","earn"]},{"id":"13fcf575","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Roughly 300 nights a year, when the cold air descending from the Andes Mountains meets the warm air rising from Venezuela’s coastal Lake Maracaibo, the result is a spectacular lightning storm, its strikes so bright, so localized, and so ______ that it has become known as “Maracaibo’s Lighthouse.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Roughly 300 nights a year, when the cold air descending from the Andes Mountains meets the warm air rising from Venezuela’s coastal Lake","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Maracaibo, the result is a spectacular lightning storm, its strikes so bright, so localized, and so ______ that it has become known as “Maracaibo’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Lighthouse.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["dependable:","dependable;","dependable","dependable,"]},{"id":"b7363ba2","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Mathematician and meteorologist Edward Lorenz used the metaphor of the “butterfly effect” to explain how seemingly minor events can have major impacts on future weather. According to Lorenz’s metaphor, the wind from a butterfly flapping ______ in Brazil might eventually grow into a storm elsewhere across the globe.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Mathematician and meteorologist Edward Lorenz used the metaphor of the “butterfly effect” to explain how seemingly minor events can have","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"major impacts on future weather. According to Lorenz’s metaphor, the wind from a butterfly flapping ______ in Brazil might eventually grow into a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"storm elsewhere across the globe.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["its wings","its wings’","it’s wing’s","it’s wings’"]},{"id":"61160f0a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Author Madeline L’Engle, ______ to create a suspenseful tone that draws the reader in, begins her novel A Wrinkle in Time with descriptions of “wraithlike shadows” and “the frenzied lashing of the wind.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Author Madeline L’Engle, ______ to create a suspenseful tone that draws the reader in, begins her novel A Wrinkle in Time with descriptions of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“wraithlike shadows” and “the frenzied lashing of the wind.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["looked","looks","is looking","looking"]},{"id":"dd087f31","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie’s 2013 novel Americanah chronicles the divergent experiences of Ifemelu and Obinze, a young Nigerian couple, after high school. Ifemelu moves to the United States to attend a prestigious university. ______ Obinze travels to London, hoping to start a career there. However, frustrated with the lack of opportunities, he soon returns to Nigeria.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie’s 2013 novel Americanah chronicles the divergent experiences of Ifemelu and Obinze, a young Nigerian couple, after","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"high school. Ifemelu moves to the United States to attend a prestigious university. ______ Obinze travels to London, hoping to start a career there.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"However, frustrated with the lack of opportunities, he soon returns to Nigeria.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Meanwhile,","Nevertheless,","Secondly,","In fact,"]},{"id":"4223d4a6","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 1965, Yale University historians claimed that a world map called the Vinland Map was drawn in the fifteenth century. Since that time, the map’s age has been the subject of debate. In 2021, researchers conducted a study to analyze the elemental composition of the map’s ink. Their analysis revealed that the ink contains a titanium compound not used in inks until the 1920s. The researchers concluded that the map was drawn in the twentieth century. The student wants to present the study and its findings.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 1965, Yale University historians claimed that a world map called the Vinland Map was drawn in the fifteenth century.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Since that time, the map’s age has been the subject of debate.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 2021, researchers conducted a study to analyze the elemental composition of the map’s ink.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Their analysis revealed that the ink contains a titanium compound not used in inks until the 1920s.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The researchers concluded that the map was drawn in the twentieth century.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Given the debate about the Vinland Map’s age, researchers in 2021 conducted a study to analyze the elemental composition of the map’s ink.","A 2021 study of the Vinland Map’s ink revealed that it contains a titanium compound not used in inks until the 1920s, indicating that the map was drawn in the twentieth century.","The Vinland Map, believed by some to have been drawn in the fifteenth century, was the focus of a 2021 study.","Aware that a certain titanium compound was not used in inks until the 1920s, researchers in 2021 studied the elemental composition of the Vinland Map’s ink."]},{"id":"fc5e83cc","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"When following musical scores, professional opera singers like soprano Ana María Martínez take vocal directions from descriptive notations, typically in Italian, that appear alongside the musical notes. ______ these descriptive terms might guide the performer to sing giocoso (playfully) or lento (at a slow tempo).","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"When following musical scores, professional opera singers like soprano Ana María Martínez take vocal directions from descriptive notations,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"typically in Italian, that appear alongside the musical notes. ______ these descriptive terms might guide the performer to sing giocoso (playfully)","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"or lento (at a slow tempo).","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["On the other hand,","All the same,","For example,","In the second place,"]},{"id":"eaded344","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The painter Frida Kahlo is one of the most influential artists of the twentieth century. She was born in Coyoac án, Mexico, in 1907. She is best known for her vivid and richly symbolic self-portraits. The Two Fridas (1939) features two versions of Kahlo sitting together. One version wears a European-style dress and the other a traditional Tehuana dress. The student wants to introduce Kahlo to an audience unfamiliar with the artist.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The painter Frida Kahlo is one of the most influential artists of the twentieth century.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She was born in Coyoacán, Mexico, in 1907.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"She is best known for her vivid and richly symbolic self-portraits.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Two Fridas (1939) features two versions of Kahlo sitting together.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"One version wears a European-style dress and the other a traditional Tehuana dress.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Known for being vivid and richly symbolic, Frida Kahlo’s self-portraits include The Two Fridas (1939).","The 1939 painting The Two Fridas is one example of a self-portrait by Frida Kahlo.","One painting by Frida Kahlo features two versions of herself, with one version wearing a European-style dress and the other a traditional Tehuana dress.","One of the most influential artists of the twentieth century, Mexican painter Frida Kahlo is best known for her self-portraits, which are vivid and richly symbolic."]},{"id":"52b31d7b","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In November 1934, Amrita Sher-Gil was living in what must have seemed like the ideal city for a young artist: Paris. She was studying firsthand the color-saturated style of France’s modernist masters and beginning to make a name for herself as a painter. ______ Sher-Gil longed to return to her childhood home of India; only there, she believed, could her art truly flourish.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In November 1934, Amrita Sher-Gil was living in what must have seemed like the ideal city for a young artist: Paris. She was studying firsthand","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the color-saturated style of France’s modernist masters and beginning to make a name for herself as a painter. ______ Sher-Gil longed to return to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"her childhood home of India; only there, she believed, could her art truly flourish.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Still,","Therefore,","Indeed,","Furthermore,"]},{"id":"25361ec6","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Scientists long debated the origins of chondrules, tiny glass beads that formed in meteors billions of years ago. For decades, different theories were proposed, from lightning strikes to powerful rock collisions, but none had sufficient evidentiary support. ______ scientists found strong evidence that chondrules were formed by shock waves in nearby nebulae.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For example,","Therefore,","Similarly,","Finally,"]}];
